      <div class="card">
        <ul class="quickref-list">
          <li>D&amp;C = design STRATEGY (not an algorithm): Divide → Conquer → Combine.</li>
          <li>Needs: similar subproblems, independent solving, efficient combining. Heavy overlap → Dynamic Programming.</li>
          <li>Base case stops recursion; recursive case shrinks the problem.</li>
          <li>Recursion ≠ D&amp;C. Smaller problems ≠ less total work.</li>
          <li>Recursion tree of Merge Sort: height log2 n, ~n work per level → O(n log n).</li>
          <li>Binary Search: sorted input, Divide only, no Combine, O(log n) time, O(1) space; 1,000,000 → ~20 comparisons.</li>
          <li>Merge Sort: Divide O(1), Combine O(n); O(n log n) in ALL cases; O(n) auxiliary space.</li>
          <li>Quick Sort: pivot + partition, in-place, no merge; avg O(n log n), worst O(n^2) (bad pivot / sorted input); avg space O(log n).</li>
          <li>Divide phase is similar across algorithms; the Combine phase differs.</li>
          <li>Advantages: simplifies, improves asymptotics, parallelizable. Limits: call-stack memory, costly combine, not for every problem.</li>
          <li>Next lecture: Recurrence Relations + Master Theorem.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
